---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { page, readOnlyFlags, results, working } from "../lib/results";
import SetupPrompt from "../components/SetupPrompt.astro";

const { totals } = results;
const onlyRead = totals.got_past_policy === readOnlyFlags;
const scored = results.decision.filter((model) => model.pairs > 0);
const sum = (value: (model: (typeof scored)[number]) => number) => scored.reduce((total, model) => total + value(model), 0);
const answers = [
  { tone: "status-ok", word: "Pass", command: "ls -la", text: "Your harness's own rules decide." },
  { tone: "status-warn", word: "Needs approval", command: "az vm list", text: "Claude Code asks you. Yes runs the command, and no doesn't. Other harnesses stop it, because an ignored question would let it run." },
  { tone: "status-bad", word: "Stop", command: "sudo ls", text: "The command stops, and nobody is asked." },
];
---

<Base title="termite" description="termite finds shell commands and scripts with AI models on your own machine, and it checks them for you and your agents. It never runs them.">
  <div class="hero">
    <div>
      <h1>Find and check shell commands with local AI models</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        termite uses AI models on your own machine to find shell commands and scripts, and it
        checks each one before you use it. termite never runs these commands, and it can stop
        agents from running destructive ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        It supports both sides of computer security: the blue team, who defend systems, and the
        red team, who attack systems to find weaknesses first.{" "}
        <a href={page("docs/plain-english/")}>Read the introduction in plain English</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <img class="demo" src={page("assets/ask.gif")} width="1200" height="600"
      alt="Typing a question mark and a request at a zsh prompt; termite explains a find command and asks Y or n; approving it puts it at the prompt, and it does not run" />
  </div>

  <section class="team blue">
    <h2>Blue team: get safe commands</h2>
    <p>Ask for a command in plain English, using AI models on your own machine. termite treats every model as untrusted: it explains each command, marks risky commands in red, and never runs anything.</p>
    <p><a href={page("docs/getting-started/")}>Install termite</a></p>
    <div class="pair">
      <section>
        <h3>At your prompt</h3>
        <dl class="uses">
          <dt><code>? what is using my disk space</code></dt>
          <dd>One command, explained. If you approve it, it goes to your prompt.</dd>
          <dt><code>?? what is on port 8080</code></dt>
          <dd>Every sensible way to do it, explained. Choose one, ask again or cancel.</dd>
          <dt><code>?x show disk usage</code></dt>
          <dd>Injected at your prompt with no question, if it only reads and every check passes.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p>These examples use the optional prompt widget for zsh, bash or fish. Without it, <code>tmite</code> prints the same result in any shell. Your requests and folders stay on your machine. <a href={page("docs/using-termite/")}>Read the full guide</a>.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>For your agents</h3>
        <p>A hook checks each shell command before your agent runs it. The hook never approves a command. It gives one of three answers:</p>
        <dl class="uses">
          {answers.map((answer) => (
            <dt><span class:list={["status", answer.tone]}>{answer.word}</span> <code>{answer.command}</code></dt>
            <dd>{answer.text}</dd>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p><a href={page("docs/agents/#commands-that-need-approval")}>See what each harness does</a>.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="pair next">
  <section class="team setup">
    <h2>Set it up with your agent</h2>
    <p>Copy this prompt and give it to any agent, such as Claude Code or Codex. The agent finds your system, your harnesses and the models that fit. You type y for each change in your own terminal, and then the agent proves that the checks work. <a href={page("docs/agents/")}>Read about agent tools</a>.</p>
    <SetupPrompt folded />
  </section>

  <section class="team red">
    <h2>Red team: test a model</h2>
    <p>Test an AI model before you trust it. termite sends the model hostile requests, records which requests get a dangerous command, grades the result, and shows you what to block or restrict.</p>
    <p><a href={page("docs/red-team/")}>Test a model</a></p>
  </section>
  </div>

  <h2>What the tests found</h2>
  <p>
    I tested {working.length} command models, which write commands, and
    {" "}{results.decision.length} decision models, which score how risky a command is.
    {" "}<a href={page("results/")}>Read the test results</a>.
  </p>
  <ul class="found">
    <li><a href={page("results/#wrote")}>What the command models wrote</a>: {totals.commands_written} commands, and {totals.hazardous} of them were hazards.</li>
    <li><a href={page("results/#did")}>What termite did with the commands</a>: {totals.got_past_policy === 0
      ? `none of those ${totals.hazardous} hazards could reach your prompt without approval.`
      : onlyRead
        ? `${totals.got_past_policy} could reach your prompt without approval, and these only read a file.`
        : `${totals.got_past_policy} could reach your prompt without approval.`}</li>
    <li><a href={page("results/#judged")}>How the decision models judged risk</a>: the riskier folder got the higher score in {sum((model) => model.folder_right)} of {sum((model) => model.folder_pairs)} tests.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { display: grid; gap: 1.4em 3em; align-items: center; }
  .demo { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 6px; }
  .team { border-top: 4px solid; padding-top: 0.4em; margin-top: 3em; }
  .team h2 { margin-top: 0.4em; }
  .team.blue { border-color: #3f8cff; }
  .team.red { border-color: #ef4d4d; }
  .pair { display: grid; gap: 0 3em; }
  .pair section { min-width: 0; }
  .uses { margin: 0.8em 0; }
  .uses dt { margin-top: 0.9em; }
  .uses dd { margin: 0.2em 0 0; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 60ch; }
  .team.setup { border-color: var(--line); }
  .next .team { margin-top: 3em; }
  .found { padding-left: 1.2em; }
  .found li { margin: 0.4em 0; }
  @media (min-width: 60em) {
    .hero { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 6fr); }
    .pair { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  }
</style>
